{:edb/db-before-t 1078 :edb/db-after-t 1079 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534391 50 #inst "2026-10-06T23:49:00.223Z" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087567 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/875/Source/Images/e77c2a7f7fa0a1f9a45e1aa139b3a543.png)" 13194139534391 false] [17592186087567 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generate the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/0ae3d46a511cfe74b489eec33e6f6fec0adcb5be7d955a1880f979963b0e01f6.png)" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087567 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0)$, and passes through the point $(4,8)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = 2pt; 8 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= 2pt \\\\\n2 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{2}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= p(\\frac{2}{p})^{2} \\\\\n8 &= p \\cdot \\frac{4}{p^{2}} \\\\\n8 &= \\frac{4}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2\n$$" 13194139534391 false] [17592186087567 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = 2pt, \\qquad y = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = 2\\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t, \\qquad y = \\left(\\dfrac 12 \\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac 12 t^2 .\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087568 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x$?" 13194139534391 false] [17592186087568 1100 "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $y^2 = -64x?$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087568 1145 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4px &= -64x \\\\\n4p &= -64 \\\\\np &= -16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t\n$$" 13194139534391 false] [17592186087568 1145 "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $y^2=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx = pt^2, \\qquad y = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $y^2=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-64x \\\\ 4p & =-64 \\\\ p & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx = (-16)t^2, \\qquad y = 2(-16)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx = -16t^2, \\qquad y = -32t .\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087569 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087569 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^2, \\qquad y = 4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139534391 false] [17592186087569 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{x}{2}, t = \\frac{y}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= \\frac{y^{2}}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= \\frac{x}{2} \\\\\ny^{2} &= 8x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x$." 13194139534391 false] [17592186087569 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{x}{2},\\,t=\\frac{y}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = \\dfrac{x}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is $y^2 = 8x.$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186087741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285790 13194139534391 true] [17592186087741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285811 13194139534391 true] [17592186087741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285840 13194139534391 true] [17592186087741 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285847 13194139534391 true] [17592186087742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285797 13194139534391 true] [17592186087742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285819 13194139534391 true] [17592186087742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285824 13194139534391 true] [17592186087742 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285830 13194139534391 true] [17592186087743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285804 13194139534391 true] [17592186087743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285816 13194139534391 true] [17592186087743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285836 13194139534391 true] [17592186087743 1020 #edb/ref 17592186285854 13194139534391 true] [17592186088373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534391 true] [17592186088373 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186088373 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x =-\\frac{1}{4}t, y =-\\frac{1}{8}t^{2}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139534391 false] [17592186088373 1145 "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-4x,\\,{t}^{2}=-8y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =16{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-8y$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2} & =-8y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation of the parabola is ${x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}y.$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186088374 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534391 true] [17592186088374 1100 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)$?" 13194139534391 false] [17592186088374 1100 "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-3,-3)?$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186088374 1145 "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-3,-3),$ we can substitute $x=-3$ and $y=-3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3=2pt \\\\ -3=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ -3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{9}{4p} \\\\ 4p & =-3 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{3}{4})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{3}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}t,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186088376 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534391 true] [17592186088376 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/8e85666a72dde1ebbe24ff3d8dbb1404f010e81d9406aec790be11416b705f00.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534391 true] [17592186088376 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/875/Source/Images/q-84110.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$, generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534391 false] [17592186088376 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,-6).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(3,-6),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=-6$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -6=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =2pt \\\\ -3 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(-\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(3){t}^{2},\\,y=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=3{t}^{2},\\,y=6t.\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1017 #edb/ref 17592186285791 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1089 "q-106412" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997987/assets/c42f5aac98d31b7a66a60eebae34b5e83cb62f3ba4801595a81dd9bb57706384.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1113 #uuid "827bc944-9468-5f1f-aaa1-d206a7a58e66" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285790 1145 "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-8),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -8=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285792 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285793 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285794 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285795 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1000 #edb/ref 17592186285796 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1041 "selection" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1104 #edb/ref 17592186285792 13194139534391 true] [17592186285791 1110 #uuid "c8795698-e693-5154-b430-5eaeb64341d6" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285792 1034 "x=-t,\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534391 true] [17592186285792 1111 #uuid "3afa95b7-02e2-5ada-bc15-1419124e03ef" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285793 1034 "x=-2t,\\,\\,y=-{t}^{2}" 13194139534391 true] [17592186285793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534391 true] [17592186285793 1111 #uuid "5e742002-4a38-5ead-a25e-c6f22